{"type":"user","uuid":"01a0a9a1-4365-7fd7-822d-7233388671fa","session_id":"01a0a9a1-0884-7083-ab3b-7bcef8753b24","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_01a0a9a117ea72e690abc1be","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"pnpm default store location\",\n  \"totalResults\": 16,\n  \"showing\": 10,\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/settings/store\",\n      \"title\": \"Store & Lockfile Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"# Store & Lockfile Settings\\n\\n## Store Settings\\n\\n### storeDir\\n\\nThe location where all the packages are saved on the disk.\\n\\nThe store should be always on the same disk on which installation is happening,\\nso there will be one store per disk. If there is a home directory on the current\\ndisk, then the store is created inside it. If there is no home on the disk,\\nthen the store is created at the root of the filesystem. For\\nexample, if installation is happening on a filesystem mounted at `/mnt`,\\nthen \",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.826278,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/global-virtual-store\",\n      \"title\": \"Global Virtual Store | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"# Global Virtual Store\\n\\nBy default, pnpm creates a `.pnpm` directory inside each project's `node_modules` — this is the \\\"virtual store\\\". It contains hardlinks to files in the content-addressable store. Every project gets its own projection of this virtual store — pnpm hardlinks files from the content-addressable store into the `.pnpm` directory structure. The actual file contents exist only once on disk, but the directory structure is recreated for each project so that Node.js's module resolutio\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.8016413,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/faq\",\n      \"title\": \"Frequently Asked Questions | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"If you run `pnpm install` on disk `A`, then the pnpm store must be on disk `A`.\\nIf the pnpm store is located on disk `B`, then all required packages will be\\ndirectly copied to the project location instead of being linked. This severely\\ninhibits the storage and performance benefits of pnpm.\\n\\n`pnpm install`\\n`A`\\n`A`\\n`B`\\n\\n### Store path is NOT specified\\n\\nIf the store path is not set, then multiple stores are created (one per drive or\\nfilesystem).\\n\\nIf installation is run on disk `A`, the store will \",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.77792513,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/cli/store\",\n      \"title\": \"pnpm store | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"When the global virtual store is enabled, `pnpm store prune` also performs mark-and-sweep garbage collection on the global virtual store's `links/` directory. Projects using the store are registered via symlinks in `{storeDir}/v11/projects/`, allowing pnpm to track active usage and safely remove unused packages from the global virtual store.\\n\\n`pnpm store prune`\\n`links/`\\n`{storeDir}/v11/projects/`\\n\\n### path\\n\\nReturns the path to the active store directory. [...] For example, during `pnpm install`\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.6783488,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/settings/node-modules\",\n      \"title\": \"Node-Modules & Hoisting Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"`pnpm add -g`\\n`pnpm dlx`\\n\\nWhen enabled, `node_modules` contains only symlinks to a central virtual store, rather than to `node_modules/.pnpm`. By default, this central store is located at `<store-path>/links` (use `pnpm store path` to find `<store-path>`).\\n\\n`node_modules`\\n`node_modules/.pnpm`\\n`<store-path>/links`\\n`pnpm store path`\\n`<store-path>`\\n\\nIn the central virtual store, each package is hard linked into a directory whose name is the hash of its dependency graph. As a result, all projects on\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.852752,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/10.x/configuring\",\n      \"title\": \"Configuring | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Configuring | pnpm\\n\\n# Configuring\\n\\npnpm uses npm's configuration formats. Hence, you should set configuration the same way you would for npm. For example,\\n\\n```text\\npnpm config set store-dir /path/to/.pnpm-store\\n```\\n\\nIf no store is configured, then pnpm will automatically create a store on the same drive. If you need pnpm to work across multiple hard drives or filesystems, please read the FAQ.\\n\\nFurthermore, pnpm uses the same configuration that npm uses for doing installations. If you have a priv\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/global-packages\",\n      \"title\": \"Global Packages | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"`pnpm install -g`\\n`pnpm add -g <pkg>`\\n\\n## Binaries location\\n\\nGlobally installed binaries are stored in a `bin` subdirectory of `PNPM_HOME` (i.e., `$PNPM_HOME/bin/`). This keeps the `PNPM_HOME` directory clean — internal directories like `global/` and `store/` don't pollute shell autocompletion when `PNPM_HOME` is on PATH.\\n\\n`bin`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n`$PNPM_HOME/bin/`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n`global/`\\n`store/`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n\\nAfter upgrading to pnpm v11, run `pnpm setup` to update your shell configuration so that `$PNP\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.71377134,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/configuring\",\n      \"title\": \"Configuring | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Configuring | pnpm\\n\\n# Configuring\\n\\npnpm settings are divided into two categories:\\n\\n- Authentication and certificate settings are stored in INI files. These contain sensitive credentials and should not be committed to your repository. See Authentication Settings for details.\\n- All other settings are stored in YAML files: the project `pnpm-workspace.yaml` and the global `config.yaml`.\\n\\npnpm also no longer reads settings from the `pnpm` field of `package.json`. Settings should be defined in `pnpm-w\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/settings/other\",\n      \"title\": \"Other Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"### globalBinDir\\n\\nAllows to set the target directory for the bin files of globally installed packages.\\n\\nIn pnpm v11, globally installed binaries are stored in a `bin` subdirectory of `PNPM_HOME` instead of directly in `PNPM_HOME`. This prevents internal directories like `global/` and `store/` from polluting shell autocompletion when `PNPM_HOME` is on PATH. After upgrading, run `pnpm setup` to update your shell configuration.\\n\\n`bin`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n`global/`\\n`store/`\\n`PNPM_HOME`\\n`pnpm se\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.6659544,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/cli/cache-path\",\n      \"title\": \"pnpm cache path | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"pnpm cache path | pnpm\\n\\n# pnpm cache path\\n\\nAdded in: v11.22.0\\n\\nPrints the directory pnpm uses for its metadata cache, mirroring `pnpm store path`. The path is printed absolute and lexically cleaned, so a relative `cacheDir` yields a path other tools can consume.\\n\\npnpm cache path \\n\\npnpm derives this directory from the platform and the `cacheDir` setting, so a CI setup that wants to cache it no longer has to mirror that resolution by hand:\\n\\n.github/workflows/ci.yaml\\n\\n- name: Get the cache director\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"0b3ccc929d054966902934ca289463fb\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"accepted\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": false\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    \"By default, pnpm's virtual store is located in `<store-path>/links`. The store is typically created on the same disk as the project. The exact path can be found with `pnpm store path`.\",\n    \"The default pnpm store location is determined by your operating system and environment variables, typically placing the store on the same disk as your project to enable hard linking [1][2].\\n\\nIf no custom configuration exists, the default paths are:\\n- Linux: ~/.local/share/pnpm/store\\n- macOS: ~/Library/pnpm/store\\n- Windows: ~/AppData/Local/pnpm/store\\n\\nIf the $PNPM_HOME environment variable is set, pnpm uses $PNPM_HOME/store. If the $XDG_DATA_HOME variable is set, it uses $XDG_DATA_HOME/pnpm/store. To identify the exact path currently in use, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npnpm store path\\n```\"\n  ],\n  \"answersAvailable\": true,\n  \"providerAnswers\": [\n    {\n      \"provider\": \"tavily\",\n      \"text\": \"By default, pnpm's virtual store is located in `<store-path>/links`. The store is typically created on the same disk as the project. The exact path can be found with `pnpm store path`.\",\n      \"verified\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"provider\": \"exa\",\n      \"text\": \"The default pnpm store location is determined by your operating system and environment variables, typically placing the store on the same disk as your project to enable hard linking [1][2].\\n\\nIf no custom configuration exists, the default paths are:\\n- Linux: ~/.local/share/pnpm/store\\n- macOS: ~/Library/pnpm/store\\n- Windows: ~/AppData/Local/pnpm/store\\n\\nIf the $PNPM_HOME environment variable is set, pnpm uses $PNPM_HOME/store. If the $XDG_DATA_HOME variable is set, it uses $XDG_DATA_HOME/pnpm/store. To identify the exact path currently in use, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npnpm store path\\n```\",\n      \"verified\": false,\n      \"citations\": [\n        {\n          \"url\": \"https://pnpm.io/settings/store\",\n          \"title\": \"Store & Lockfile Settings | pnpm\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"url\": \"https://pnpm.io/